Let $a=2001$. Consider the set $A$ of all pairs of integers $(m,n)$ with $n\neq0$ such that
(i) $m<2a$;
(ii) $2n|(2am-m^2+n^2)$;
(iii) $n^2-m^2+2mn\leq2a(n-m)$.
For $(m, n)\in A$, let \[f(m,n)=\frac{2am-m^2-mn}{n}.\]
Determine the maximum and minimum values of $f$.

Determine all functions $f: \mathbb{Q} \rightarrow \mathbb{Z} $ satisfying 
\[ f \left( \frac{f(x)+a} {b}\right) = f \left( \frac{x+a}{b} \right) \]
for all $x \in \mathbb{Q}$, $a \in \mathbb{Z}$, and $b \in \mathbb{Z}_{>0}$. (Here, $\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$ denotes the set of positive integers.)

Let $\mathbb{N} = \{1,2,3, \ldots\}$. Determine if there exists a strictly increasing function $f: \mathbb{N} \mapsto \mathbb{N}$ with the following properties:

(i) $f(1) = 2$;

(ii) $f(f(n)) = f(n) + n, (n \in \mathbb{N})$.

Find all functions $f: \mathbb{R}^2 \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$, such that
1) $f(0,x)$ is non-decreasing ;
2) for any $x,y \in \mathbb{R}$, $f(x,y)=f(y,x)$ ;
3) for any $x,y,z \in \mathbb{R}$, $(f(x,y)-f(y,z))(f(y,z)-f(z,x))(f(z,x)-f(x,y))=0$ ;
4) for any $x,y,a \in \mathbb{R}$, $f(x+a,y+a)=f(x,y)+a$ .

Define the sequences $(a_n),(b_n)$ by
\begin{align*}
& a_n, b_n > 0, \forall n\in\mathbb{N_+} \\ 
& a_{n+1} = a_n - \frac{1}{1+\sum_{i=1}^n\frac{1}{a_i}} \\ 
& b_{n+1} = b_n + \frac{1}{1+\sum_{i=1}^n\frac{1}{b_i}}
\end{align*}
1) If $a_{100}b_{100} = a_{101}b_{101}$, find the value of $a_1-b_1$;
2) If $a_{100} = b_{99}$, determine which is larger between $a_{100}+b_{100}$ and $a_{101}+b_{101}$.

Whether there are integers $a_1$, $a_2$, $\cdots$, that are different from each other, satisfying:
(1) For $\forall k\in\mathbb N_+$, $a_{k^2}>0$ and $a_{k^2+k}<0$;
(2) For $\forall n\in\mathbb N_+$, $\left| a_{n+1}-a_n\right|\leqslant 2023\sqrt n$?

For each integer $a_0 > 1$, define the sequence $a_0, a_1, a_2, \ldots$ for $n \geq 0$ as
$$a_{n+1} = 
\begin{cases}
\sqrt{a_n} & \text{if } \sqrt{a_n} \text{ is an integer,} \\
a_n + 3 & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
$$
Determine all values of $a_0$ such that there exists a number $A$ such that $a_n = A$ for infinitely many values of $n$.

